Raw dev notes -- RAG Faithfulness & The Data Grind

This project is all about making an AI reliably answer questions based on a specific, dense corpus of ancient texts – think of it like a super-powered study buddy for complex religious scholarship. The core challenge is ensuring the AI's answers are *faithful* to the source material, not just plausible-sounding hallucinations. We're using a Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) approach, so it's all about getting the right text snippets to the language model.

This past week or so has been a whirlwind of diagnostics, iteration, and a lot of data cleanup. A big early win was finally nailing down *why* some answers were wrong. For a while, I was convinced the generator was just making stuff up, but after digging into a garbled answer (which turned out to be a simple data entry error in our gold standard, oops!), the diagnosis became clear: it was a **retrieval gap**. The model wasn't hallucinating; it just wasn't *finding* the right information, especially for secondary sub-parts of questions. This was a huge shift in focus.

To tackle this, we built out some crucial calibration tools. We started showing the retrieved sources directly in our `grade.html` evaluation interface and saving user's first-pass notes. Later, we added a dedicated question-source panel and a way to re-review the top-10 retrieved sources. These tools were invaluable for understanding the retrieval failures.

The biggest breakthrough on the retrieval front was `v21: per-section retrieval`. Instead of trying to retrieve at a higher level (like a whole page or "daf"), we broke the text down into smaller, more atomic sections. This proved incredibly effective, recovering buried paragraphs and significantly boosting faithfulness (+0.028, which is huge in this context). We quickly promoted v21 to production. Interestingly, a follow-up attempt, `v22 per-section rerank`, actually performed *worse* and was rejected. Sometimes simpler is better, I guess.

A significant chunk of time also went into refining our external baseline and the "pure corpus" itself. We had to re-classify all 539 questions using a batch LLM process, which was a pain due to output limits – had to split the request into 8 chunks! Then came the real grind: `pure-corpus ref recovery`. This involved a lot of manual triage, surveying PDFs, and even using vision models to extract text from images to ensure our "pure corpus" (the set of documents we *know* contain the answers) was as complete and accurate as possible. We cleaned up the 293 set, recovered 41 missing references, and saw another bump in faithfulness from 0.729 to 0.746. It's amazing how much impact data quality has.

Finally, we implemented `atomized eval (#133)`, which allows us to evaluate faithfulness at a per-sub-part level on the pure-corpus. This gave us a much more granular view, showing a flat 0.857 faithfulness, which is a great baseline to work from.

Honestly, this period felt like a constant battle between trying to be clever with models and just doing the grunt work of data hygiene. The initial misdiagnosis about hallucination versus retrieval gap was a humbling reminder to always question assumptions and build good diagnostic tools. Seeing the faithfulness numbers tick up after all that effort, especially with the per-section retrieval and the pure-corpus cleanup, is incredibly satisfying. It reinforces the idea that RAG performance is often more about the quality of your retrieval and your data than just throwing a bigger LLM at the problem.

Next up: Deep dive into the atomized evaluation results to pinpoint specific areas for improvement.
